{
    "Insight Presentation & Visualization": {
        "analysis": "Comparing the visualizations between the candidate and baseline reports on the three sub-dimensions:\n\n1. **Visual clarity & professionalism (Candidate: +3, Baseline: 0)**: The candidate report demonstrates superior visual clarity and professionalism. It features multiple well-structured charts with clear titles, proper axis labeling, appropriate color schemes (consistent use of blue/orange for problem/normal groups), and clean layout. The charts appear to be from a modern visualization library with appropriate spacing and alignment. The baseline report has only one chart (scatter plot) which, while functional, lacks the polish and consistency of the candidate's multiple visuals. The candidate's systematic use of visualization throughout the report enhances readability and professional appearance.\n\n2. **Chart appropriateness & accuracy (Candidate: +2, Baseline: 0)**: The candidate report shows better chart appropriateness for each analytical task. It uses: bar charts for comparing CTR/conversion rates between groups, bar charts for cost-per-conversion comparison, bar charts for match type distribution, box plots for IMI/TQS distributions, and bar charts for top campaigns. Each chart type matches the data relationship being shown (comparison, distribution). The baseline report relies primarily on a single scatter plot with color coding by match type, which is appropriate for showing CTR vs. conversion correlation but lacks the comprehensive visualization of multiple dimensions. The candidate's charts appear to have appropriate scales and avoid misleading representations.\n\n3. **Text–visual synergy & insightfulness (Candidate: +2, Baseline: 0)**: The candidate report excels in explicitly referencing and explaining each visualization. Each chart is introduced with context (\"This chart visualizes the core discrepancy...\") and followed by interpretation that connects the visual to the insight. The narrative highlights key patterns visible in the charts rather than merely restating numbers. The baseline report mentions its scatter plot once but provides minimal textual guidance about what to observe in the visualization. The candidate's approach helps readers quickly grasp insights from the visuals, while the baseline's visualization feels more decorative and disconnected from the narrative.\n\nOverall, the candidate report significantly outperforms the baseline in all three sub-dimensions, with more comprehensive, professional, and well-integrated visualizations that enhance understanding of the insights.",
